analysisWe need to read the java code and find the comment that starts with "@@@". It's inside set(int fromIndex, int toIndex, boolean value) method.

Comment: 
```
// @@@ If the index range is valid, every bit in the range [fromIndex, toIndex) is set to the specified value after the operation.
```

We need to produce an assert statement that corresponds to this natural language assertion.

We need to use public observer methods of the class. In this class, public methods include: cardinality(), get(int), get(int, int), isEmpty(), length(), nextClearBit(int), nextSetBit(int), previousClearBit(int), previousSetBit(int), size(), toByteArray(), toLongArray(), toString(), maybe others not listed but we can use get(bitIndex) to check each bit.

The method set(int fromIndex, int toIndex, boolean value) sets bits from fromIndex (inclusive) to toIndex (exclusive) to value. It calls set(... ) or clear(... ) accordingly, which presumably sets bits individually. The assertion: If the index range is valid, every bit in the range [fromIndex, toIndex) is set to the specified value after the operation.

We need to express "if the index range is valid". What constitutes valid? According to the method's Javadoc: throws IndexOutOfBoundsException if fromIndex negative, toIndex negative, or fromIndex larger than toIndex. So valid range means fromIndex >=0 && toIndex >=0 && fromIndex <= toIndex. Also probably toIndex can be equal to fromIndex (empty range). So we need to assert: (fromIndex >=0 && toIndex >=0 && fromIndex <= toIndex) => (forall int i; fromIndex <= i && i < toIndex; this.get(i) == value). Use \forall int i; condition; spec.

We need to reference value variable (boolean). Use value directly.

Thus final assert: 
assert (fromIndex >= 0 && toIndex >= 0 && fromIndex <= toIndex) => (\forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < toIndex; this.get(i) == value);

Make sure parentheses for => lower precedence than &&. So we need parentheses around left side maybe: (fromIndex >=0 && toIndex >=0 && fromIndex <= toIndex) => (\forall int i; i>=fromIndex && i<toIndex; this.get(i) == value);

That matches pattern.

Return statement is void, so no \result.

Thus final answer.

